// @test: compile-fail
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, aliases, protocols, project, T1073
// @timeout: 60000
// @project: aliased_protocol
// @expected-exit: 101
// @skip: the harness's `compile-fail` path does not honour `@project:`.
//        Measured 2026-09-03 on one binary: `verum check`, `verum build`
//        and `verum run` on this very directory all report
//        `error<E427>: `show` does not match the signature `Shower`
//        declares` and exit non-zero (101/101/1), while vtest reports
//        "Compilation unexpectedly succeeded". The toolchain is right and
//        the runner cannot see it. `typecheck-fail` is not an option:
//        the runner refuses `@project` for it — "only supported for
//        `run`, `run-panic` and `compile-fail`". So the NEGATIVE pole of
//        a multi-file project is unobservable by the suite today; T1114.
//        The skip comes off when the runner runs what the user runs, not
//        by weakening the expectation.
// @description: Mounting a protocol under an alias must not disable the
//   conformance checks.
//
//   Measured 2026-09-03, before the fix (T1073):
//
//       mount probe.lib.{Shower};          E427 reported
//       mount probe.lib.{Shower as S};     E427 SILENT
//
//   The impl in the fixture is wrong either way — the protocol declares
//   `-> Int` and the impl returns `Bool`. The alias silently disabled
//   THREE checks at once (GAT arity, the associated-type bound, and this
//   signature check), because they all ask the registry by the name AS
//   WRITTEN and a miss returns no diagnostics.
//
//   Not a wrong answer — a hole, and it opened on the spelling a user
//   reaches for precisely when two modules collide, which is when the
//   checks matter most. The resolver already existed:
//   `canonical_alias_target` walks the alias chain, and it had one caller.
//
//   WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM THE FIXTURE (T1113). The T1073
//   commit added `aliased_protocol/` — verum.toml, src/lib.vr,
//   src/main.vr — and no spec. The directives lived at the top of
//   `src/main.vr` and named `@test: check`, which is not one of the
//   suite's test types. Discovery therefore found a spec it could not
//   read, and refused the WHOLE directory:
//
//       vtest run specs/L2-standard/modules
//       Error: 1 spec(s) could not be read, so they were neither run nor
//       counted
//
//   So the check this fixture was written to pin ran nowhere, and it took
//   every other spec in the directory down with it. Its healthy siblings
//   (`two_file_project/`, `module_leaf_collision/`) carry NO directives
//   inside `src/`, which is why they are invisible to discovery — the
//   directives belong in a spec file beside the project, never inside it.
//
//   `compile-fail` rather than `typecheck-fail`: the fixture is a cog, so
//   the subject is a directory and the run is a full `verum build`, and
//   the author's own expectation was a non-zero exit.

fn main() {
    // The program lives in the sibling directory named by `@project:`.
    // This body is never compiled — vtest hands the compiler that
    // directory, not this file.
}
